    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 142018\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n118 PM MST Mon Sep 14 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Abundant moisture will remain situated across the eastern half\n  of Arizona providing enhanced chances for showers and \n  thunderstorms through the middle portion of the week, especially\n  over the mountain locations.\n\n- There will be the potential for some storms to produce strong winds,\n  but the bigger threat will be heavy rainfall leading to \n  localized flash flooding over the next few days.\n\n- Shower and thunderstorm chances will diminish by the end of the \n  week through the upcoming weekend as drier air filters in. \n\n- Near to slightly above normal temperatures are expected throughout \n  the entire week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Wednesday/...\nLatest objective analysis indicates the subtropical ridge of high \npressure centered over the southeastern states while broad upper-\nlevel troughing remains situated over the northwestern states. This \noverall pattern configuration continues to support more of a south \nto southwest flow across much AZ while more westerly flow, bringing \ndrier air, is occurring from the Lower Colorado River Valley \nwestward. The south to southwest flow entrenched across much of AZ \nis expected to remain in place through the middle of the week, \naiding with periodic moisture influxes as PWAT values peak above \n1.5\" along with low-level mixing ratios of 12-14 g/kg. Therefore,\nan elevated potential for shower and thunderstorm activity will \nremain in place, especially from Phoenix eastward through the \nmiddle of the week before chances diminish towards the end of the \nweek once the drier air filters in.\n\nThe combination of plentiful moisture and strong daytime heating \nshould result in good instability once again as MUCAPE values are \nexpected to peak at 1000-1500 J/KG. The latest HREF membership shows \nsoutheast AZ becoming convectively active during the afternoon hours \nwith some of this activity drifting into the higher terrain areas to \nthe east and southeast of Phoenix with more isolated activity across \nthe south-central AZ lower deserts. Similar to the last couple of\ndays, despite the very favorable thermodynamic parameters in \nplace, the lack of good upper-level forcing for ascent continues \nto be a limiting factor for more widespread activity. With these \nstorms, heavy rainfall leading to localized flash flooding will be\nthe primary threat with strong downburst wind gusts a secondary \nthreat given that DCAPE values will still be sufficiently high, \nranging between 1000-1200 J/KG. Quieter conditions can be \nexpected this evening into at least the first part of the \novernight period, before potentially another round of elevated \nshowers and storms develops, most likely after 3 AM MST. The \nlatest hi-res guidance is indicating the best potential over the \nfoothills and higher terrain to north and east of Phoenix. \nHowever, the convective threat late in the overnight hours into \nearly Tuesday morning will be highly conditional of whether or not\na gravity wave/outflow will be a sufficient triggering mechanism \nfor development as the overall larger scale forcing for ascent \nwill still be lacking.\n\nBetter upper-level support is more likely to materialize, especially\nheading into late Tuesday through Wednesday, as a weak mid-level \nlow currently located over the southern tip of the Baja Peninsula \nin conjunction with another weak upper-level low off the west \ncoast of the Baja Peninsula move into the region. Therefore, these\nfeatures are likely to pair up and be sufficient to trigger more \nwidespread shower and thunderstorm activity across much of central\nand eastern AZ. Latest NBM is honing in on this increasing \ncoverage as it shows PoPs increasing to 50-80% from Phoenix \neastward from Tuesday evening through Wednesday. The main concern \nwill be an increasing threat for flash flooding given the abundant\nmoisture in place with both the ECMWF and GFS indicating PWATs \nexceeding 1.8\" along with low-level mixing ratios as high as 14 \ng/kg. In addition, MLCAPE values are likely to range between \n1000-1500 J/KG providing plenty of instability. Therefore, the \nenvironment is likely to become favorable for these thunderstorms \nto produce high rainfall rates in excess of 1-2\"/hr, leading to \nthat enhanced flash flood potential. A Flood Watch will likely be \nneeded for portions of the area from late Tuesday through \nWednesday.\n\nTemperatures through the middle of the week will continue to run \nslightly above normal across the western deserts as high top out \naround 105 degrees, while more clouds and potential rainfall will \nkeep highs closer to 100 degrees across the south-central AZ lower\ndeserts. In fact, with the greater likelihood of more abundant \ncloud cover as well as higher rainfall potential, afternoon highs \non Wednesday across much of the south-central AZ lower deserts \nare now forecast to top out in the 90s. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Thursday through Sunday/...\nGuidance is starting to agree on a quicker drying trend later this\nweek as the Pacific trough is favored to move farther into the\nGreat Basin and at least into northern and western portions of the\nDesert Southwest. This is likely to push drier air eastward\nthrough most if not all of Arizona as early as Thursday but more\nso Friday into Saturday. Thursday is still a bit of an uncertain\nday as rain chances may still linger across the south-central\nlower deserts and definitely over eastern Arizona. However, by\nFriday, enough dry air moving in should end chances over the \nlower deserts and really limit chances over the high terrain. It \nis doubtful we will completely scour out all of the moisture \nacross the higher terrain, so 10-20% PoPs remain each day across \neastern Gila County through the weekend. \n\nAs conditions dry out into the weekend, temperatures are likely \nto face a bit of a warming trend but as of now the region is \nfavored to stay under broad upper level troughing into the \nweekend. Lower desert highs are expected to warm to above normal \nby Friday or Saturday, but so far the NBM indicates a most likely \nrange of 103-107 degrees. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1735Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \n\nThe main impact will be periods of VCSH/VCTS, and SHRA/TSRA later\nthis afternoon (00-04Z) and once again tomorrow morning (10-14Z).\nConfidence remains low as early morning models had a similar \noutlook this morning, with nothing developing as expected. However,\nthe environment looks more favorable over the next couple of days\nbut monitoring and updates will need to follow. SHRA/TSRA will be\ncapable of producing locally gusty winds with a low chance \n(10-20%) for gusts >30 kts. A period of southerly cross winds will\ngive way to westerly flow by this afternoon (21-22Z) then back \nE/SE tonight. Wind speeds will mostly stay under 10 kts, outside \nof shower and storm influences. SCT to occasionally BKN cloud \ndecks around 8-10K ft AGL will be common through the TAF period.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \n\nNo aviation weather concerns will exist through the TAF period\nunder mostly clear skies. Winds will continue to behave similar \nto the past 24 hours varying between southeasterly and westerly at\nKIPL and southerly to southwesterly at KBLH with periods gusts\nbetween 20-25 kts this afternoon. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nPeriods of scattered showers and thunderstorms are likely through\nWednesday across the eastern districts supporting a localized \nheavy rainfall threat. Moisture levels will remain elevated except\nfor southeast California. Expect minimum humidity levels to still\nfall into a 15-25% range for the western districts while the \neastern districts will only dip to 30-45% through Wednesday. \nWinds will generally follow diurnal patterns across the eastern \ndistricts, while much of the western districts will have elevated \nsoutherly winds with occasional gusts above 20 mph. By Thursday\ninto Friday, much drier air is forecast to spread eastward across\nthe region, eventually ending rain chances for all but the far\neastern Arizona high terrain and lowering humidities into the\nteens across the lower deserts.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman \nAVIATION...Ryan \nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
